{
 "id": "2026-zhao-trees",
 "name": "Trees, Naturalness and Activity Willingness – Photo Ratings",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the paper's own stimuli: Zhao et al. (2026), \"More trees, more naturalness, and greater willingness to engage in physical activity: an image-based study\", Frontiers in Public Health 14: 1881655. Participants see eye-level photographs of everyday streets and open spaces in Yucheng District, Ya'an, China, in a fully random order. For each photo they rate perceived naturalness on a 4-point scale without a midpoint (very unnatural, somewhat unnatural, somewhat natural, very natural) and their willingness to do physical activity such as walking or group exercise there on an 11-point scale (0 = completely unwilling, 10 = very willing). Ratings are keyed by photo; the file names carry the paper's vegetation level (low / medium / high), so image-level means can be related to tree, grass and other-plant proportions from semantic segmentation, as in the paper. In the original study (337 university students, 40 photos), naturalness and activity willingness correlated r = 0.949 at image level and visible tree proportion best identified spaces seen as natural. Differences from the original study: (1) only the 12 photographs printed in Figure 1 (4 per vegetation level) are included, not all 40; (2) the images are cropped from the published figure, so they are small and the top strip carrying the figure labels is removed; (3) the paper ran on Wenjuanxing in Chinese, this template is in English; (4) age and sex are asked at the start.",
